st":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,2331,12,16,15,7546,400,77,3,646,387,15251,438,400,18,17,4306,3,304,825,2331,323,400,17,18,880,3,304,2441,2331,11,1380,400,64,3,323,400,65,3,525,894,7546,23092,8131,1091,220,18,13,3555,374,279,24632,3204,2629,400,64,3,31719,65,3,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA base-10 integer $n$ can be represented as $32_a$ in one base and $23_b$ in another base, where $a$ and $b$ are any integer bases larger than 3. What is the smallest possible sum $a$+$b$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"3058e9f1dbd98762523726199269db7741d4603d77f867b748d33c19f1f7e3ba"},"seconds":11.805986166000366,"seed":595947467845916410,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to express the base-10 integer \\( n \\) in terms of the bases \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). The number \\( 32_a \\) in base \\( a \\) can be converted to base-10 as:\n\\[ n = 3a + 2. \\]\nSimilarly, the number \\( 23_b \\) in base \\( b \\) can be converted to base-10 as:\n\\[ n = 2b + 3. \\]\nSince both expressions represent the same integer \\( n \\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[ 3a + 2 = 2b + 3. \\]\nRearranging the terms, we get:\n\\[ 3a - 2b = 1. \\]\nWe need to find the smallest possible values of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) that satisfy this equation, with the conditions that \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are integers greater than 3. We can solve this equation using the method of finding particular solutions and then the general solution.\n\nFirst, we find a particular solution. By inspection or trial, we find that \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( b = 4 \\) is not a solution because \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) must be greater than 3. Trying \\( a = 5 \\):\n\\[ 3(5) - 2b = 1 \\implies 15 - 2b = 1 \\implies 2b = 14 \\implies b = 7. \\]\nSo, \\( a = 5 \\) and \\( b = 7 \\) is a solution. To confirm, we substitute back:\n\\[ 3(5) + 2 = 17, \\]\n\\[ 2(7) + 3 = 17. \\]\nBoth expressions equal 17, so \\( n = 17 \\), and the values \\( a = 5 \\) and \\( b = 7 \\) are correct.\n\nThe smallest possible sum \\( a + b \\) is:\n\\[ 5 + 7 = 12. \\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{12}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{12}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:66"}
